          "title": "You'll want to know: exemplum, exemplī",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "exemplum, exemplī — \"an example; a warning\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Pater exemplum bonum fīliō dedit. The father set his son a good example."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "exemplum, exemplī n. Exemplī grātiā, for the sake of an example, gives English e.g.; exemplī causā means the same. Exemplum facere is to make an example of someone."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "exemplum comes from eximō \"to take out\" (EtymDB; Lewis & Short), built from ex and emō, whose old sense was \"to take\": a piece taken out to show the rest. English example and sample come from it through French."
            }
          ]
        },
